<section class="service-page-intro">
  <div><p class="eyebrow">Information design</p><h2>Choose the form because of what it needs to explain.</h2></div>
  <div class="prose"><p>Visualisation is most useful when it makes a relationship visible that would otherwise be slow or difficult to understand. That starts with identifying the question: whether the audience needs to compare quantities, follow a process, understand a mechanism or decide where to act.</p><p>We interrogate the source material, test possible structures and build a visual whose level of detail fits the audience and context. The answer may be a familiar chart, a bespoke diagram or a sequence of simpler views rather than one overloaded graphic.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="section-shell service-offers">
  <div class="split-heading"><h2>Different questions need different visual tools<span class="red-stop">.</span></h2><p>The useful form is the one that makes the intended comparison or relationship easier to see.</p></div>
  <div class="service-offer-grid">
    <article><span>01</span><h3>Data visualisation</h3><p>Charts and evidence displays built around accurate comparison, useful annotation and a clear takeaway.</p></article>
    <article><span>02</span><h3>Processes and timelines</h3><p>Visual sequences that clarify stages, dependencies, decisions, delays and points of intervention.</p></article>
    <article><span>03</span><h3>Systems and mechanisms</h3><p>Causal maps and explanatory diagrams for situations where several factors interact over time.</p></article>
    <article><span>04</span><h3>Decision tools</h3><p>Matrices, frameworks and structured views that support prioritisation, workshops and practical action.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="service-practical">
  <div><p class="eyebrow">What the work can include</p><h2>From analytical question to a usable visual system.</h2></div>
  <ul class="service-checklist"><li>Source review and visual scoping</li><li>Chart selection and data hierarchy</li><li>Process, system and causal diagrams</li><li>Maps, timelines and logic models</li><li>Decision frameworks and risk matrices</li><li>Reusable visual styles within your brand</li></ul>
</section>

